WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:07.440
This is somewhere no human-being was meant to go.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.120
Deep in the foothills of the Himalayas,&nbsp;
temperatures near Reasi vary in the extremes

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.400
dropping from 50 degrees celsius&nbsp;
all the way down to freezing.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.520
Everywhere you look, jagged cliff faces&nbsp;
create these deep, dangerous canyons.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.240
You can imagine the challenge faced by crews

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.200
attempting to build a world&nbsp;
record breaking bridge here.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.760
To access civilisation, you have to trek the

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.840
dusty tracks by foot or take a&nbsp;
boat journey along the river.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.800
Simply getting to the Chenab Bridge&nbsp;
site was an enormous undertaking&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.040
let alone manoeuvring the 30,000&nbsp;
tons of steel necessary to build it.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.520
No motorways, no nearby factories and&nbsp;
not a flat, stable surface in sight.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:01.860
This is construction in one of mother&nbsp;
nature’s most unforgiving environments.

00:01:04.812 --> 00:01:08.880
It might sound cliche but&nbsp;
sometimes cliches do ring true:

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.880
constructing Chenab Bridge&nbsp;
should have been impossible.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:20.400
From searing temperatures, to dangerous ice and&nbsp;
snow, working conditions don’t come much tougher than this

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.800
and it’s not just the crews who suffer&nbsp;
- when exposed to extreme temperatures

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.200
steel expands and shrinks making it a real&nbsp;
challenge to manipulate and accurately line up

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.920
but the truth is while the crews are battling the snow and the sun,&nbsp;there’s an even bigger threat looming and that is the wind.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.240
You see, this canyon experiences powerful&nbsp;
gusts of up to 165 miles an hour.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.560
Now, that’s unbelievably dangerous for&nbsp;
people working hundreds of metres up the&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.760
mountains as it is, let alone when&nbsp;
you consider the fact that they’re&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.520
manoeuvring thousands of tons of steel into place.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.120
And even if the weather decides&nbsp;
to take it easy on you from above

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.423
there’s no guarantee an earthquake&nbsp;
won’t shake the ground to its core.

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:05.520
Any one of these&nbsp;
factors in their own right could

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.360
have put a stop to the notion of building a&nbsp;
canyon-crossing bridge here and still they

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:18.320
pale in comparison to the main challenge&nbsp;
of Chenab Bridge: actually getting to the construction site.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.131
When we first started the project we were invited&nbsp;to go up and donkeys were provided to help us get&nbsp;up the stream.

00:02:24.131 --> 00:02:30.970
David Mackenzie is the senior technical director for COWI and has been involved with the project since 2004.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.011
The first&nbsp;trip on the way up we took a boat on the way out because it

00:02:34.011 --> 00:02:38.720
was quicker by boat down the river than it was to&nbsp;
try and negotiate these tortuous mountain paths.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.640
Ok, so this is why Chenab Bridge is being&nbsp;
constructed in the first place.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.680
Imagine you’re in Jammu and you&nbsp;
want to get over to Kashmir Valley.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.560
They’re separated by mountains and connected by

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.560
one main artery - National Highway 44&nbsp;
which is regularly blocked by snow.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.560
That’s where Chenab Bridge comes in&nbsp;
- it’s just one impressive section of

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:06.000
India’s new 169 mile all-weather rail line&nbsp;
cut quite literally through the mountains

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.800
and as if the conditions weren’t challenging&nbsp;
enough, not everybody supports this new route.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.000
Kashmir is an area of high&nbsp;
political tension and it’s

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.120
one of the most militarised zones in the&nbsp;
world, divided between India and Pakistan.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.680
While India says its new&nbsp;
all-weather rail line will&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.040
give the area an economic boost&nbsp;
and improve travel connections

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.240
opponents claim it’s being used as a way&nbsp;
of exerting more control over the region.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.120
But despite these concerns, construction&nbsp;on the line has plowed on. All in all this thing features an incredible

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:48.387
943 bridges and 36 tunnels,&nbsp;
including one stretching 7 miles long

00:03:48.387 --> 00:03:53.920
and apparently all in, reports suggest it's gonna cost a mere $5 billion.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:02.251
For context on that, in New York, $5 billion&nbsp;
gets you two miles of Second Avenue Subway.

00:04:03.931 --> 00:04:08.800
Now, when seemingly every project we look at costs millions&nbsp;
if not billions of dollars, it’s&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.120
easy to lose perspective over the&nbsp;
scale of money that's being talked about.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.920
$2 billion dollars on a skyscraper&nbsp;
here, $10 billion dollars on

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.320
a railway over there and sometimes these&nbsp;
projects can run into the trillions.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.320
Balancing those chequebooks would bring most of us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.520
out in a cold sweat. I actually feel&nbsp;
a bit sick even thinking about it.

