Now, from managing your money&nbsp;
to managing a mega-bridge in

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.040
the Himalayas, let's get back to Chenab.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.400
This is the Chenab Bridge worksite in 2020&nbsp;
and on either side of the canyon you’ll notice

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.680
these fabrication workshops. You wouldn’t&nbsp;
usually find them on modern construction sites.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.520
Usually on a project this big, key&nbsp;
components would be prefabricated in

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.640
factories far away and manoeuvred to site but&nbsp;
as we mentioned that wasn’t an option here.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.120
Right about now, you're probably asking yourself,&nbsp;
‘if it’s so hard to get to this site

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.480
how did they manage to get&nbsp;
30,000 tons of steel up there?’

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.160
The answer is pretty extraordinary. To make this location even&nbsp;
remotely accessible

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.880
they put in 26km of&nbsp;
roads just for this project.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.240
But if you’re prone to travel&nbsp;
sickness you might want to look away now

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.746
because these roads were built in some pretty precarious locations

00:06:21.746 --> 00:06:26.720
clinging to the side of mountains and going along some serious cliff edges.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.920
The most efficient way of transporting steel is&nbsp;
in flat plate. Think of it as an IKEA catalogue

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.760
everything came to site flat packed and indeed all&nbsp;
the cutting and all the drilling was done on site.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.680
And don’t expect things to get much&nbsp;
easier once you’ve reached the site.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.080
Once you finally have all of the materials&nbsp;
on location, then comes the small issue&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.600
of building the bridge hundreds of metres&nbsp;
above the ground, over a giant canyon.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.320
The topography here is nothing short of a&nbsp;
nightmare - a jagged, rocky canyon split&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.040
by a river makes for some beautiful views&nbsp;
but an incredibly tricky engineering job.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.600
As I mentioned earlier, one of&nbsp;
the biggest factors is the wind

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.280
thanks to a phenomenon&nbsp;
known as the Venturi effect.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.560
That’s when a narrow passage&nbsp;
like a canyon becomes a giant funnel.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.880
As wind passes through, its pressure&nbsp;
drops as its speed increases

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.400
creating powerful and chaotic patterns
and that’s before we’ve discussed the terrain.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.440
In geological terms, the Himalayas are&nbsp;
very young and they’re still moving.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.160
The range was forced up by the Indian and&nbsp;
Eurasian plates driving into one another

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.560
creating a heavily twisted landscape.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.960
The rock along its canyons&nbsp;
is fractured and friable.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.760
Now, by nature a canyon is a kind&nbsp;
of ‘v’ shape and the problem is, if you try to fix a

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:51.600
tower to one side of that 'v' shape, particularly one side that has very unstable rock conditions, there’s a tendency&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:57.200
for that tower to want to slide down the valley and slip&nbsp;
which is why Chenab Bridge is an arch design.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.400
It starts from either end

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.560
with both sides meeting in the middle&nbsp;
and creating a locked shape like this.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:05.760
As they meet in the middle,&nbsp;
the two sides push against each other

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.560
forcing weight down through&nbsp;
the legs and into the mountain.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.600
So, I mentioned that a canyon&nbsp;
acts a bit like a wind funnel

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.920
creating these powerful gust patterns.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.160
Usually, you’d model the structure

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.200
in this case a bridge, and put a scaled&nbsp;
down version to the test in a wind tunnel

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.960
and while that was done here&nbsp;
with about a 1 to 50 scale model

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.280
the unique topography called&nbsp;
for more drastic research.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.400
What we then did were some localised tests both&nbsp;
physical tests but also computational tests so

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.360
we model the environment around it, the hills, the&nbsp;
valleys so then we can actually calculate what are

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.000
the accelerated flows locally because of course&nbsp;
they push up in their wind speed so if there’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.000
acceleration of 20% your maximum wind speed of&nbsp;
say, 50 metres per second is now 60, 70 metres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.800
per second so you’ve gotta take those things into&nbsp;
account and that’s the important thing that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.120
done here was to do those particular studies&nbsp;
because they were very important to the design.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.240
Now, the ability to stabilise this bridge&nbsp;
with an incredibly strong arch is crucial.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.800
But it’s stretching over a canyon, hundreds&nbsp;
of metres above the river down below.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.680
To actually build it, crews had&nbsp;
to first construct a cable crane.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.600
It’s a bit like an aerial trapeze&nbsp;
or a monorail made of cables.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.600
Towers are constructed on either side&nbsp;
of the canyon and a cable connects them.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.120
Pieces of steel for the arch are winched across&nbsp;
on the cable crane and into the correct location.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.320
Each side of the arch is then&nbsp;
built from the bottom upwards

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.280
held back by cables locked into the&nbsp;
rock to stop them toppling nearly 400&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.720
metres down to the river below and once&nbsp;
complete, hey presto, you have an arch.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.760
The arch is the key component&nbsp;
supporting the entire bridge

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.760
structure but it’s obviously not&nbsp;
fit for a train to cross just yet.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.760
Once the supporting towers are erected

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.480
that’s when the top deck comes&nbsp;
into play and it’s a pretty heavy component.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.720
The top deck is a flat piece on&nbsp;
the bridge that’s constructed in sections.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.960
Basically, working from each side of the canyon

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.120
a piece of top deck is laid, then&nbsp;
another piece is pushed in behind it

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.737
and another piece behind that and so and so on and gradually&nbsp;the bridge deck is

00:10:08.737 --> 00:10:12.960
pushed out across the canyon until the two sides meet in the middle.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.720
It’s known as a launch methodology.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:21.360
What’s cool is that this structure is so&nbsp;
big, the scale of earthquakes generally

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.920
experienced here shouldn’t be too much of an&nbsp;
issue in comparison but that’s more than just

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:33.120
a stroke of luck - engineers were well&nbsp;
aware the area faces seismic activity.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.400
When you get bridges of this&nbsp;
size, curiously if you imagine

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:40.160
the ground is shaking but in proportion&nbsp;
to the size and span of the structure

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:44.800
that shaking is actually quite small.&nbsp;
If you halve the span, relatively that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.360
shaking is twice as much because the shaking&nbsp;
is a fixed amount so the ground shakes by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:55.120
let’s say plus or minus 10cms. But if the bridge&nbsp;
is 100 metres long, if you double it to 200 metres&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.280
then of course the relative effect diminishes&nbsp;
so actually the seismic is actually less of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.440
impact on the main arch but it does affect&nbsp;
all of the rest of the approach structures.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.400
It goes without saying the stability of the top&nbsp;
deck is crucial - the safety of trains has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.040
be central to the project’s construction and one&nbsp;
of the key environmental threats to the bridge's

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:20.240
stability is experienced around the world -&nbsp;
although maybe less so in the UK: the sunshine.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.920
If you were paying attention, you’ll remember&nbsp;
I said right at the start of this video that

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.560
temperatures at Chenab Bridge vary from&nbsp;
freezing all the way up to a scorching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.600
50 degrees celsius and that impacts the&nbsp;
steel that the bridge arch is made out of.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:37.120
The big problem with a canyon is that&nbsp;
the sun can only shine on sections of&nbsp;

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.880
the bridge at any one time, meaning it’ll&nbsp;
need to operate at different temperatures.

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.160
As the bridge arch warms and cools, it bends&nbsp;
and flexes - not great for moving trains.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.259
To get around that, the rails have been pre-stressed, they’ve been pulled really tight for stability.

00:11:53.259 --> 00:11:59.676
They're also allowed to move freely of their fixings on the bridge. So, the fixings on the bridge have the rails sitting in them 

00:11:59.676 --> 00:12:04.605
but they're not actually attached so the rails can move back and forth as the bridge moves over time.

00:12:06.035 --> 00:12:11.760
Designed by the WSP team in Finland, the&nbsp;
arch and its towers loom over Chenab River.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:15.600
The rock and steel blend in with the&nbsp;
mountainous backdrop and in doing so

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:19.520
offer some of the best train&nbsp;
passenger views on the planet.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.520
I've been very fortunate to work on a number&nbsp;
of very exciting structures in my lifetime&nbsp;

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:30.000
but this one holds particular passion for me&nbsp;
because having seen it from ground up over a&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.728
20 year period, having seen where it's been built, the quality with which it's been built as well

00:12:34.728 --> 00:12:38.880
it’s incredible how well it’s been&nbsp;
fabricated in the yard. It really is an incredible&nbsp;

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.440
piece of engineering and I think just looking at&nbsp;
the pictures as I am on my screen in front of me

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.600
it really does tug at the heart strings&nbsp;
of being the challenge I’m most interested&nbsp;

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.325
in and the proof is I have it on my laptop&nbsp;
everyday to look at it.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:58.880
The line was inaugurated in summer last year as&nbsp;
India begins its next chapter of connectivity

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.760
and while Chenab bridge is just one part of&nbsp;
a number of rail projects across the country

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.240
it might be the most impressive.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:11.280
To create this bridge, construction teams have&nbsp;
had to build where no one has built before

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.960
and yet through determination and&nbsp;
innovation, they've made it a reality.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.920
More than 20 years after construction first began

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.480
following delays and complications,&nbsp;
boat journeys and trekking on donkeys

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:30.480
cable cranes and miles of purpose built&nbsp;
roads, construction crews found a way.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:37.200
The Chenab Bridge is the perfect example of&nbsp;
the hardships faced by construction teams working in&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.640
some of our planet's most extraordinary environments.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.440
The enormous efforts invested to create&nbsp;
structures like this pave the way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:53.440
future generations and proves once again what the amazing construction&nbsp;industry is capable of.

